A||||2||Cheever3000||One more thing from TeamCheever.com||||14||
Z||000000||Cheever3000||10-15-2001||11:17 PM||||Recently, <A HREF="http://www.teamcheever.com" TARGET=_blank>www.teamcheever.com</A>  held a contest for fans to submit a 250-word-maximum "story or anecdote" relating to Eddie.  I submitted my story of taking my family to the 98 Indy 500, and what a perfect day it was for us in many ways.  I agonized over my entry for more than a week, getting everything perfect and cutting whatever I could to get it under 250 words.  The winner was announced today, and it wasn't me, but I don't mind.  It was over 250 words, but I don't mind that much either.  Here it is.  It doesn't take long to read it. I hope you will...<P>"My stepson James was an Eddie Cheever fan. In October or November 1998, I found the Team Cheever web site and sent a letter to request an autographed picture for James. Within two weeks the picture came back along with an autographed picture of Robby Unser. Both were personalized to James. I can’t tell you the thrill on his face as he tore into the envelope from Team Cheever. He always said that Eddie was his personal friend; getting the pictures was confirmation that he mattered. He carried that autographed picture with him to Emory University for his bone marrow transplant. The signature became a little smudged as it went through the sterilization process but he didn’t care. He carried it so much that it became torn and frayed, still he didn’t care. And with each person that asked him about it, he would smile and simply said that his friend Eddie had sent it to him. It always sparked conversation and he never tired of singing praises for Eddie and the IRL even when he was so weak that he could not sit up. One of James’ last wishes was to go to an IRL race. He was in the stands for the Charlotte race in May 1999. I am thankful that he did not fully understand what happened there that night. His wish was to meet Eddie, only by that time he could not have made it to the Pits. Still he was there and that is what remains important. James was eight years old and had leukemia. After a long and courageous battle, he passed away in August 1999. We buried James with his two prized possessions, the tattered picture and a signed Daytona 500 hat. In today’s world of sports stars that are so oblivious to the fans, it amazes me the things that Eddie and the Cheever Indy Racing staff do for the children. Little hearts are so fragile. Thank you, Eddie Cheever, for remembering the children. You are to be commended for the charity work that you do, for spending so much time with children both on and off the track and for continuing to be a constant presence in your own children’s lives. James was a little boy caught in the middle of his parent’s war. He did not understand divorce. He instead chose his heroes in people he would never meet. Although you never met him, he called you his friend and I am thankful that you did not treat his request lightly and that someone in your office sent the pictures. In life it is too easy to not take time to tell people how much the little things matter. It is true that you don’t know if the last time you see someone will truly be the last time you see them. So I guess by sharing this story as I have done many times, it is keeping a part of James alive. You made him smile when he was really down and for that, I am grateful."<BR> ||||reg||14
Z||000001||hugh||10-16-2001||07:36 PM||||Chuck,Kinda makes you feel that this isn't such a bad world after all doesn't it?||||reg||1
Z||000002||MrPBody||10-16-2001||11:09 PM||||Thank you for posting that, even though I find it painful to read.  I work for Children's Healthcare of Atlanta (Egleston / Scottish Rite).  We see some brave, amazing kids.<BR>||||reg||1
